The animation stopped partway (a partial line). So the DrawScreen's rAF loop stopped. Why? Because each click resets `exampleProgress` and restarts... no, it's not restarting.

Look at the loop:

```js
start() {
  this.active = true;
  this.resize();
  const loop = (now) => {
    if (!this.active) return;
    this.t += 1/60;
    if (this.exampleAnimating) {
      this.exampleProgress = Math.min(1, this.exampleProgress + 0.018);
      if (this.exampleProgress >= 1) this.finishExample();
    }
    this.render();
    this.raf = requestAnimationFrame(loop);
  };
  cancelAnimationFrame(this.raf);
  this.raf = requestAnimationFrame(loop);
}
```

Our fake rAF returns a timeout ID (a number in the browser). Then `cancelAnimationFrame(this.raf)` (native) does nothing. Fine.

But wait: our override calls `setTimeout(() => cb(performance.now()), 2)` — no recursion problem. Hmm, but did the override break with `stop()`? startDrawing might call `drawScreen.start()` each time? Let's look at startDrawing. Also, is this the *fast* test? The partial line suggests ~14 frames then stopped. 450ms at 2ms → 225 frames, should be done.

Could the line be the animation from the *latest* click only 450ms in? No, the animation runs at 0.018/frame → 55 frames = 110ms.

Unless setTimeout is throttled heavily in the background/headless browser (timer throttling to 1s in background tabs). In Playwright with a visible browser window, probably not. But with `setTimeout(2)` maybe Chrome clamps to 4ms after nested timers → still fine.

Hmm, but our rAF override affects other code that expects rAF to be tied to frames. The draw screen's `render()` may be called, but not progressing.

Actually — WAIT. The issue might be that the DrawScreen's start loop is not using the overridden rAF because `addInitScript` overrides `window.requestAnimationFrame`, and the module's `requestAnimationFrame` resolves to the global. That should work.

Let me check whether `finishExample` is even reached: exampleProgress = 0.018 * frames. In the screenshot the line is ~35% complete → ~20 frames. So the loop ran ~20 times in 500ms+ and then stopped. SetTimeout throttling? 20 frames in ~1.4s ≈ 14fps. Hmm, that's the real rAF rate! So the override didn't take effect. The ~35% progress matches ~20 frames at 8-14fps in ~2s.

So the override wasn't applied: maybe because `page.addInitScript` runs before page scripts, but the module's `requestAnimationFrame` references... In ES modules, `requestAnimationFrame` is a global property lookup on window. If boot captured it? No.

Hold on: the DrawScreen loop uses `this.raf = requestAnimationFrame(loop)` — it resolves window.requestAnimationFrame at call time. Unless the browser caches the native? No.

Maybe addInitScript ran in a different context (file page? no, after page.goto to http). Hmm.

Let me test with a simpler approach: after clicking, manually drive the animation by calling `requestAnimationFrame` many times? The loop schedules itself via the fake or native.

Instead, wait longer: at ~8fps, each animation takes ~7s. 7 clicks × 8s = 56s. That's acceptable (with a 120s timeout). But it'd be better to verify quickly. I can also check whether the analysis completes eventually with one or two clicks and take a few extra seconds.

Alternatively, override `window.requestAnimationFrame` *after* load via page.evaluate, before clicking. The module's loop calls the global on each frame, so the next frame should use the fake. The DrawScreen loop is started on startDrawing (after the override). Let me do:

```js
await page.evaluate(() => { window.requestAnimationFrame = (cb) => setTimeout(() => cb(performance.now()), 2); });
```

But the main game loop has already captured `requestAnimationFrame`? It calls the global each time too. This will make the game loop run at 500fps, which could bog the browser with renders; on the draw screen, the main loop only renders the showroom if appState==='menu' (it's 'draw' here), so no rendering. Good.

Let me retry with the override applied via evaluate after load, and then check. If it's still slow, just wait longer.

Also, I notice the canvas is 680 wide but the draw screen's `readout` shows 0m — because analyze hasn't run yet.

Let me modify the test: after clicking #btn-start, evaluate the override, then loop clicks. Also increase the wait to 900ms and check the progress. Let me also read `#toast` (the "Loading X…" toast is shown) — the final "Inspiration loaded" toast only shows when onExampleDone runs; both use the same toast element, so the notice text will change. Good.

Actually, `toast(msg)` probably displays for a fixed time. Fine.

Let me test.

